# A(l), the absorber map: which of the three structural buffers each layer says took up its
# remainder. One row per layer that names an absorber and whose named term a reader has mapped.
#
# An unmapped taxonomy has no row
#   A layer citing a taxonomy nobody has mapped has no row, and that is correct: the question is
#   unanswerable, not answered zero.
#
# The mapping is a reader's assertion
#   The join through `buffer_term` is a reader's assertion and not a document's. The absorber
#   travels as a taxonomy plus a value, so a Portuguese filing citing a translated edition of
#   Factory Physics says `capacidade`. That `capacidade` is `capacity` is a judgement somebody
#   made; no filing says it. Deleting the mapping row makes the slack rules stop answering for that
#   filing rather than answer wrongly, which is the behaviour worth having.
#
# Why the absorber is not an enum
#   A restated value set is a fork, and a fork drifts with nothing able to notice. Declared as an
#   enum, this schema does not load the corpus at all: `capacidade` is not one of its members.
-- pm:Remainder/absorber, resolved through pm.buffer_term.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.absorber_taxonomy AS taxonomy,
       l.absorber_value    AS term,
       bt.buffer,
       bt.note AS the_readers_warrant
FROM pm.layer l
JOIN pm.buffer_term bt ON bt.taxonomy = l.absorber_taxonomy AND bt.value = l.absorber_value
